  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Coastal Flood advisory has been dropped at this \ntime. Nuisance flooding is still possible for mainly Downeast \nCarteret county through the next several high tide cycles but data \nfrom this morning showed significant receding compared to yesterday. \nStanding water on roadways, and in low lying or poor drainage areas, \ncould have minor impacts for travelers in especially vulnerable \nareas prone to the tidal flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...As a weak upper level trough exits the eastern seaboard \ntoday, it will be replaced by an upper level ridge with high \npressure and southerly flow at the surface. This will keep \nconditions dry but we will see a return of warmer temperatures \nand higher humidities each day, culminating with some very warm \ntemperatures by the end of the week.\n\nFor reference, normal highs in early October are around 80 degrees, \nand forecast highs are in the mid to upper 80s for much of ENC, with \nsome 90s possible inland. Persistent southerly flow and increasing \nlow-level moisture should help to keep overnight lows higher as \nwell, and well above the normal lows of around 60.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Precipitation chances return for the weekend as\nGulf moisture opens up and a cold front drapes across the \nsouthern Appalachians and pushes southeastward. This will \nsqueeze moisture into eastern NC elevating rain chances for the \nweekend. While thunderstorms will be possible the threat for \nsevere weather is low at this time. \n\nFor now, ensemble guidance is showing about a 30-50% chance of \nexceeding 1\" of rain with this system. Any rain will be helpful, \nespecially for the coastal plain of ENC that is currently in \nmoderate to extreme drought.",
